AB  - We investigated whether integrating users’ diary entries into chatbot conversations would enhance topic relevance, self-disclosure, and user evaluations. Over four weeks, we compared a retrieval-augmented generation “diary bot” referencing participants’ diaries with a non-personalized “plain bot” with 53 participants. The diary bot achieved significantly higher topic relevance, as measured by cosine similarity; however, no differences in self-disclosure emerged. Self-disclosure was also unrelated to affective states, interpersonal impressions, or willingness to use the chatbot. These results suggest that, while diary-based personalization strengthens contextual alignment, it may not increase disclosure depth in short-term interactions. This offers a scalable method for designing context-aware companion chatbots.
ER  - 

