AB  - Even as disorders like anxiety, ADHD, OCD, and stress-related problems keep increasing worldwide, timely, lowcost, stigma-free mental health care is still restricted. Even if digital mental health chatbots seem like a good solution, many of the ones that are currently available use static mappings between intentions and responses or designs based on rules, which makes the interactions shallow, the emotional sensitivity low, the personalization weak, and the contextual memory bad. Further issues with approaches based on Large Language Model (LLM) include hallucinations, poor explainability, and insufficient detection of high-risk events needing expert intervention. This research suggests an Adaptive Agentic Artificial Intelligence-driven mental health chatbot combining multi-agent systems, knowledge graph reasoning, retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), reinforcement learning, and memory-based personalization to improve dependability and user participation. The structure combines graphstructured domain knowledge using FalkorDB, vector-based retrieval with ChromaDB, a strong safety and escalation system, intent and emotion detection, condition-specific agents (ADHD, OCD, anxiety, and general wellbeing), and graph-structured domain knowledge using FalkorDB. Knowledge graph-based replies (BLEU: 0.86, ROUGE-L: 0.84, BERTScore F1: 0.98) show superior correctness, safety, and contextual relevance. Early mental health screening and individualized self-help therapies can often benefit from the proposed design, which often allows for scalable, flexible, and medically grounded artificial intelligence systems.
ER  - 

