AB  - Enterprises increasingly rely on Large Language Models (LLMs) for knowledge-driven applications such as clinical question answering, technical support, and legal analysis. However, conventional Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems face two major obstacles in enterprise deployment: data privacy concerns when relying on public LLM APIs and high resource requirements for on-premise deployment. This paper presents GalaxyRAG, a privacy-preserving and resourceefficient hybrid GraphRAG framework designed for secure and scalable enterprise reasoning. Built on the distributed graph database Galaxybase, GalaxyRAG unifies knowledge graphs and index graphs within a single retrieval engine, combining symbolic traversal with semantic search. A step-wise reasoning loop enables LLMs to perform multi-hop reasoning without fine-tuning, while a two-stage hybrid evaluation protocol ensures stable and semantically robust performance measurement. Across three multi-hop QA benchmarks and enterprise datasets, GalaxyRAG achieves up to 15-point accuracy gains, while our hybrid evaluation reduces the standard deviation of evaluation scores by over 50%.
ER  - 

