AB  - The growing availability of digital documents, particularly in Portable Document Format (PDF), has intensified the need for intelligent systems capable of extracting contextually relevant information in a conversational manner. Traditional keyword-based search methods often fail to capture semantic relationships, leading to inefficiency in information retrieval. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) architectures, which combine dense vector retrieval with large language model (LLM) generation, have emerged as a promising solution to this challenge. This review presents a comprehensive examination of RAG-based architectures for conversational document analysis, detailing their enabling technologies, methodological pipelines, and performance characteristics. We survey key developments in embedding models, vector search algorithms, prompt engineering strategies, and multimodal document understanding. Case studies, including the AARVIK system, illustrate the practical implementation of RAG workflows and highlight real-world performance considerations. The review further identifies persistent challenges-such as handling non-textual elements, managing persistent context, and reducing LLM latency-and proposes future research directions, including multimodal integration, domain-specific optimization, and standardized evaluation frameworks. Our analysis positions RAG-based systems as a foundational technology for the next generation of intelligent, interactive document interfaces.
