AB  - Semiconductor design workflows produce tightly coupled artifacts, including RTL source code, timing constraints (SDC), formal assertions (SVA), testbenches, and version-control history, whose semantic dependencies are typically analyzed in isolation by existing electronic design automation tools. Consequently, a change to one artifact may silently affect others, while engineers lack a unified representation for tracing relationships and reasoning about transitive impacts. This paper presents KEDA, a framework that automatically constructs a multi-artifact knowledge graph from hardware design repositories using open-source tools, including Yosys and Git. KEDA applies graph-traversal algorithms for change-impact analysis and graph-grounded retrieval-augmented generation (GraphRAG) for engineering question answering. Its knowledge-graph schema comprises 10 node types and 17 edge relations that unify RTL structure, timing constraints, formal assertions, testbenches, and commit history within a single queryable representation. Evaluation on five open-source designs comprising 38 modules and 207 controlled changes across seven change categories shows that weighted breadth-first search achieves an average recall of 0.94 for change-impact analysis, compared with 0.02 for a structural baseline, and improves F1 by 0.32 to 0.51 across the evaluated designs. For engineering question answering, evaluation on 81 questions across three designs shows that KEDA-QA achieves an evidence recall of 0.826 and evidence precision of 0.893. In comparison, the LLM-only approach retrieves no supporting evidence, while vector-based RAG achieves an evidence recall of 0.605 and precision of 0.409. These results indicate that structured cross-artifact knowledge graphs substantially improve change-impact traceability and evidence-grounded reasoning in hardware design workflows.
ER  - 

