AB  - Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) has achieved significant progress in the medical question answering (MQA) domain. However, introducing multiple relevant passages in the retrieval stage frequently leads to excessively long input sequences. In such cases, large language models (LLMs) tend to focus on the beginning and end of the context while neglecting the middle content, a phenomenon known as lost in the middle, which ultimately undermines answer accuracy. To address this issue, we proposed CKG-RAG, a novel framework based on knowledge graph (KG) and soft prompting. First, we organize the retrieved documents into a structured KG to reduce semantic redundancy. Second, unlike conventional approaches that flatten graphs into natural language sequences, we adopt a soft prompting mechanism that incorporates graph-structured information into learnable prompt vectors, guiding the model toward structure-aware reasoning. We validate the effectiveness and advancement of our framework on multiple public MQA datasets.
ER  - 

