AB  - Large Quranic Question Answering (QA) Language Model (LLM) architectures are the subject of an empirical study described in this paper. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), Experimental Bi-RAG, fine-tuned domain model, and general LLM base are the four pipelines that this study systematically develop. Our findings demonstrate that single-step RAG (Pipeline 3) with $\mathbf{9 1 {\%}}$ BERTScore, which makes use of traditional Islamic Tafsir (Tafsir Ibn Kathir and Al-Jalalayn), performs best and receives a Faithfulness rating of 4.2/5. Although Bi-RAG has theoretical potential, it introduces query transformation artefacts that lead to semantic drift (23%) and more hallucinations (18% vs. $\mathbf{1 1 {\%}})$. Automated Multi-Cluster Vector Scaling database with storage limitations is one of the contributions. With its authenticity, dependability, and practical application of delicate religious QA systems, Pipeline 3 is the gold standard. Code for Quran ILM is available at: https://github.com/whis-19/quran-ilm
