AB  - The fusion of Artificial Intelligence (AI) with traditional knowledge systems presents a promising direction for preserving and expanding access to India's rich medicinal plant heritage. Agentic AI, defined by autonomous reasoning, iterative planning, and adaptive problem solving, serves as a powerful tool for digitizing, analyzing, and sharing indigenous knowledge related to medicinal plants. This survey investigates the use of Natural Language Processing (NLP), Machine Learning (ML), Large Language Models (LLM), Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG), and Question Answering (QA) techniques in the development of intelligent systems for Indian medicinal plant knowledge. Current frameworks and successful applications in healthcare and ethnobotany are reviewed, alongside the integration challenges posed by diverse data sources and traditional wisdom. Evaluation of a single RAG based agent demonstrated high effectiveness, achieving a precision of 88.48%, recall of 88.64%, and $F 1$ score of 88.56%. In comparison, a standalone LLM achieved a precision of 80.41%, recall of 85.56%, and F1 score of 82.90%. These results reflect the potential of agentic AI to bridge ancient practices and modern science, offering actionable and context aware insights to support sustainable healthcare and biodiversity management.
ER  - 

