AB  - Generative AI tutors are attractive for universities that lack enough human tutoring capacity, but unbounded cloud chatbots are difficult to audit, costly to operate, and risky when advice is not grounded. This paper presents a cloud-edge retrieval-augmented agent tutor for trustworthy personalized learning in resource-constrained higher education. The workflow combines early-risk learning analytics, local evidence retrieval, policy checking, route-aware cloud escalation, and an explicit run record. On the Open University Learning Analytics Dataset (OULAD), the executed pipeline processed 32,593 student-course records, produced 8,149 test predictions, audited 160 high-risk feedback cases, and generated a full-test routing audit. Logistic regression achieved Macro-F1=0.757, AUC=0.838, and at-risk recall $=0.693$ from first-28-day activity; across five split seeds, Macro-F1 was $0.757 \pm 0.002$. Feature ablation, query ablation, and route-policy stress tests identify which design choices matter. The contribution is a reproducible AIoTC-style architecture and evidence package, bounded to offline public-data evaluation rather than classroom learning gains or hardware latency benchmarking.
