AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have transformed conversational agents into powerful tools for higher education, yet their use for student support and question answering remains fragmented. Institutions lack a unified view of effectiveness, architectures, and challenges such as accuracy, privacy, bias, and costs. This paper presents a Systematic Literature Review (SLR) that identifies, analyzes, and synthesizes studies on LLM-powered conversational bots in higher education. A systematic search in Scopus yielded 14 primary studies, analyzed through qualitative synthesis and descriptive statistics. Findings show predominant applications in FAQs, academic advising, and administrative support, with GPT-family models most common. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is the leading architecture for reducing hallucinations, while fine-tuning supports domain specialization. A cross-mapping of challenges and mitigation strategies reveals paired architectural and governance trade-offs. Reported effectiveness was positive, though challenges with accuracy, privacy, and costs persist. The study maps an emerging field, identifies research gaps, and proposes directions for longitudinal, cost-benefit, and ethical investigations to guide responsible chatbot integration in higher education.
