AB  - The auditability and reproducibility of answers are vital for safety and compliance in power regulation question answering. However, current approaches often lack evidence traceability, involve high costs in fine-tuning and deploying large models, and exhibit poor format adaptability. To overcome these limitations, we introduce an Evidence-First, two-stage extract-generate framework. Its first stage employs a dual-track retrieval strategy combining BM25 and vector retrieval to obtain candidate clauses, from which a fine-tuned model extracts key evidence snippets. These extracted snippets, along with associated metadata including clause numbers, versions, source URLs and confidence scores, are passed to the second stage. The second stage uses predefined templates to integrate this evidence with metadata for structured answer generation. Utilizing LORA for parameter-efficient fine-tuning, the framework updates only a minimal fraction of parameters, creating a stable retrieve-extract-generate loop. Experiments on 1069 power regulations and a DuReader-robust subset show our method achieves 88.7% evidence completeness and 89.3% format consistency under comparable resources, outperforming baselines like standard RAG and Direct LLM-RAG, while reducing risks of format collapse and citation omission. This work delivers a low-cost, high-trust technical solution for industry regulation QA with significant engineering application value.
