AB  - With the increasing sophistication of cyber attacks, traditional passive defense systems struggle to cope with stealthy offensive techniques. Although the MITRE ATT&CK framework provides a comprehensive knowledge base of adversarial behaviors, its structured and specialized nature limits its accessibility for average security practitioners. Large language models (LLMs) excel in natural language processing tasks but suffer from hallucinations, outdated knowledge, and data privacy concerns in specialized domains. To address these issues, this paper proposes a Graph Retrieval-Augmented Generation (GraphRAG) based question-answering model that integrates the ATT&CK knowledge graph with LLMs to accurately answer multi-hop reasoning questions in cybersecurity. The method constructs a knowledge graph integrating multiple sources such as ATT&CK, CVE, and CWE, and leverages LLMs for entity recognition, relation pruning, and path reasoning. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed approach significantly outperforms baseline models in F1-score, validating its effectiveness in cybersecurity knowledge question-answering.
