AB  - Traditional keyword searches offer no understa–nding of the semantics of the documents being searched, and Large Language Models (LLMs) are prone to hallucinations, making the extraction of relevant information from large PDF documents problematic. In this paper, we present DocMind, which is a framework for Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) for conversational question answering on PDF documents. The system combines semantic document parsing with token-based chunking, sentence-transformer embeddings, and HNSW-indexed vector retrieval. To find the relevant context for a document chunk, a user query is embedded in the same vector space, and the document chunk is provided to an LLM for generating a cohesive answer. DocMind, unlike standard RAG systems, has multi-turn conversational memory and a transparency feature that indicates when an answer is not justified by the document the user uploaded. Our experiments show DocMind outperforms BM25 and TF-IDF by a large margin in the BERT F1 score, ROUGE-L, and the faithfulness metric. DocMind, by semantic retrieval, combines controlled generation to enhance information accessibility and reduce hallucinations while providing reliable, transparent, and domain-agnostic question answering.
