AB  - Engineering laboratory courses in dynamics and control require students to integrate theoretical modeling, simulation, and physical experimentation while interpreting system behavior and troubleshooting implementation challenges. Providing timely and scalable instructional support in such settings remains difficult. This paper presents the design and preliminary evaluation of an AI-powered chatbot developed to support learning in a dynamics and control laboratory through instructionally guided assistance. The proposed system integrates a LLaMA-based language model with a retrieval-augmented generation framework grounded in laboratory manuals, experimental documentation, and course-specific materials. The chatbot is deployed on a private GPU server behind institutional network controls and accessed through a cloud-hosted interface, enabling secure and scalable student interaction. The system is designed to provide conceptual clarification and procedural guidance while avoiding direct solution generation. A rubric-based evaluation was conducted on 112 student-issued queries collected during the pilot deployment of a pendulum laboratory activity. Results indicate strong alignment with student intent and laboratory context, acceptable response latency for real-time use, and stable, grounded behavior under typical laboratory conditions.
