AB  - Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with knowledge graphs improves factual grounding in large language models (LLMs) but remains limited by two critical bottlenecks. First, conventional graph-based RAG models knowledge as binary triples, which fragments higher-order semantic structures and fails to preserve the integrity of n-ary relational facts. Second, non-adaptive, query-agnostic retrieval introduces redundant information that interferes with the LLM’s intrinsic reasoning. To overcome these challenges, we propose G-RAGent, a dynamic reasoning framework that unifies hypergraph-based knowledge representation with an adaptive retrieval agent. G-RAGent encodes multi-entity facts as hyperedges and employs a ReAct-style iterative reasoning process in which the LLM decomposes questions, predicts relevant semantic topics, selectively retrieves sub-hypergraphs, and halts retrieval when internal knowledge suffices. Our evaluation demonstrates G-RAGent outperforms the SOTA model Graph-CoT by 21.5 absolute points—a relative improvement of 60.9%, and reduces end-to-end latency by over 28%, demonstrating G-RAGent’s effectiveness and superiority on retrieval and reasoning.
