AB  - This paper evaluates the feasibility of deploying lightweight multimodal retrieval-augmented generation systems on resource-constrained edge devices for autonomous post-disaster analysis. We construct a specialized multimodal knowledge base from government reports and field imagery to benchmark 5 efficient open-sourced architectures: InternVL-3.5-8B, Gemma-3-4B-IT, Phi-3-Vision, Qwen2-VL-2B, and a ViLT+RoBERTa baseline. The models are evaluated using a set a question that includes both factoid and descriptive type of questions. The experimental results reveal that modern small language models significantly outperform traditional disjoint pipelines the task. The Gemma-3-4B-IT, a light-weight small language model emerged as the optimal architecture, achieving the best balance between high-resolution visual understanding and low-latency inference. It achieved an F1-score of 90.34%, and BLEU, ROUGE-L, BERT Score, SAS and average-latency of 0.34, 0.88, 0.97, 0.17 and 2.24 sec respectively.
