AB  - Visually impaired users face challenges in retrieving specific information from digital documents due to the linear design of traditional screen readers and limited contextual support. This paper introduces AccessQuery, an AI-powered Chrome extension that employs large language models (LLMs) for real-time, intelligent question-answering (QA) to enhance document accessibility. The system integrates speech recognition, natural language processing (NLP), and text-to-speech (TTS) to support multimodal and multilingual interaction with PDF content, leveraging a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) pipeline with LangChain, ChromaDB, and the DeepSeek R1 LLM for context-aware responses. Evaluation on 270 questions from 50+ academic, technical, and multilingual documents achieved strong accuracy across key metrics (BERTScore F1>0.90, precision 87.5%, recall 83.2%), while a user study with visually impaired participants confirmed high satisfaction with usability, clarity, and multilingual support. These results demonstrate the system's effectiveness in enabling non-linear, LLM-driven document navigation for visually impaired users.
