AB  - In recent years, an increasing number of AI-powered tools have been created, most of which operate as cloud-based services. The vast majority of such services upload personal notes, recordings, and documents to external servers, raising data protection and ethical concerns. The aim of the research is to design, implement and evaluate a locally executable AI-powered learning assistant capable of processing documents, images, and audio files. The system can generate answers and summaries as well as provide spoken responses through an integrated text-to-speech module. Since everything runs locally, the tool can be used completely offline. The system is built on a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) pipeline in which all source documents, the vector database, and metadata remain on the user’s machine. Several quantized LLM models were tested to find the optimal balance between accuracy and resource use on consumer hardware. The evaluation shows that a well-tuned retrieval pipeline matters more for output quality than model size alone, and that RAG significantly reduces hallucination on domain-specific Hungarian queries.
