AB  - Medical web platforms often serve users as static content repositories, unable to respond to specific health-related queries in real time. This study presents a RAG-Based (RetrievalAugmented Generation) knowledge retrieval architecture designed for integration into medical web platforms. The proposed system combines a Laravel 11 backend, ChromaDB vector database, and Llama 3.3 (70B) language model via Groq API to transform a static medical platform into an intelligent, sourcegrounded question-answering system. Medical content is chunked into 200 -word segments with 30 -word overlap, resulting in 56 chunks, embedded using all-MiniLM-L6-v2, and stored in ChromaDB for semantic retrieval. A React-based frontend component (useRAG hook) communicates with the backend API to deliver real-time responses. The system is evaluated using the RAGAS framework with the Faithfulness metric, measuring whether generated answers are grounded in the retrieved context. Results demonstrate that open-source, low-cost RAG pipelines can effectively reduce hallucination and improve information accuracy in medical web contexts.
