AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly employed for cyber threat intelligence (CTI) purposes, including attack investigations and threat actor attribution. Hallucinations, i.e., fluent but unsupported claims, present a challenge to this application, as the wrong attribution might mislead an incident response team or waste the investigative efforts. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), one of the most popular approaches to combatting hallucination by LLMs, validates the claims against the knowledge from the outside sources such as the MITRE Adversarial Tactics, Techniques, and Common Knowledge (ATT&CK) framework. The paper offers a systematic review of recent publications about RAG use in CTI question answering, retrieval-augmented systems, entailment-based claim verification in other domains, and the reliability of agentic Artificial Intelligence (AI). Throughout this literature, a persistent gap is identified: whereas the correctness and relevance of the retrieval is evaluated, none of the papers verify whether the claim is indeed entailed by the retrieved information. Notably, the analysis of one of the existing retrieval-augmented CTI tools, RAGIntel, shows that threat-actor attribution is the task where this kind of system performs the poorest, with a context precision of 0.091 compared to 0.790 for the technique classification. Moreover, even the correct threat attribution can be marked as incorrect due to the existence of aliases for threat-actor groups, e.g., "APT34" versus "OilRig". Thus, to date, among the systems identified in this review, no retrieval-augmented system has attempted to verify the attribution claims via entailment of the retrieved MITRE ATT&CK group-technique relations, taking into account possible aliases of the groups. The paper proposes to solve this gap with an alias-aware entailment-based verification layer producing an explicit statement about the relation between the claim and the evidence.
ER  - 

