AB  - Tax compliance and interpretation of fiscal regulations remain a major challenge for individuals and businesses in Pakistan, particularly due to the complexity and evolving nature of regional tax laws. While AI-powered systems offer a promising solution, existing approaches often fall short in delivering verifiable, contextually accurate, and user-tailored guidance, especially in linguistically diverse regions such as Punjab. This paper presents the design and evaluation of an AI-powered tax assistant that leverages three distinct architectures: a rule-based NLP system, a fine-tuned Large Language Model (LLM), and a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) pipeline. The proposed RAG-based approach integrates official tax documents with OpenAI embeddings and LangChain orchestration, ensuring multilingual support, source citation, and domain-specific accuracy. Extensive evaluation, including relevance scores, expert review, response latency, and user satisfaction, demonstrated the RAG model’s superiority, achieving an average relevance score of 0.83, response times under 3 seconds, and 91% expert-rated accuracy. In contrast, the rule-based and fine-tuned LLM systems lacked either contextual flexibility or traceability. The study introduces a robust citation mechanism and offers a comparative analysis of the three approaches, highlighting RAG’s effectiveness in delivering transparent, timely, and accurate tax assistance. These results underscore the transformative potential of retrieval-augmented frameworks in public sector automation and digital legal services.
