AB  - Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has emerged as a dominant paradigm for grounding large language model (LLM) outputs in factual, externally retrieved evidence. However, different RAG architectures impose fundamentally different retrieval philosophies: Native RAG retrieves unconditionally at every inference step, Self-RAG retrieves on-demand and self-critiques its own outputs using special reflection tokens, and Adaptive RAG dynamically routes each query through the simplest sufficient retrieval strategy based on query complexity. Despite the practical significance of this distinction, a controlled, multi-model empirical comparison of these three paradigms under a unified orchestration framework has not been reported. In this work, we implement all three RAG variants using LangChain and LangGraph as the orchestration backbone, with GPT-4o, Gemini 1.5 Pro, and Claude 3 Sonnet as the generator models, and evaluate them on the HotpotQA multi-hop question answering benchmark available on Hugging Face. Each pipeline is coupled with a FAISS-based dense retriever using text-embedding-3-small embeddings. Experiments are conducted on 1,000 randomly sampled distractor-setting questions from the validation split. Results show that Adaptive RAG consistently achieves the strongest balance across token F1, ROUGE-L, and RAGAS Faithfulness, while Self-RAG yields higher faithfulness scores at the cost of increased latency. Native RAG offers the lowest latency but the weakest faithfulness. Among generator models, GPT-4o outperforms Gemini 1.5 Pro and Claude 3 Sonnet on all three metrics, though the gap narrows under the Self-RAG protocol. These findings provide the first direct, framework-unified comparison of the three RAG paradigms across multiple frontier LLMs on a challenging multi-hop benchmark.
