AB  - In telecommunications and data-center services, customer messages carry different purposes such as information requests, sales intents and technical fault reports; they are often short, noisy and contain several intents at once. Routing such messages to the correct workflow, without pushing all traffic to expensive large-language-model (LLM) calls, is a critical and difficult step. We evaluate Tekna AI, a customer-interaction platform developed at Teknotel, through the lens of its architecture. The platform is layered: an incoming message is first resolved locally by a BERTurk-based classifier that also distinguishes composite intents; the suitable workflow is then selected among question answering, sales and troubleshooting. Retrieval is carried out by a hybrid layer combining HNSW dense search with BM25 lexical matching; for recurring questions, a context-aware semantic cache prevents the answer from being regenerated. The novel aspects are (i) local classification that resolves composite intents in a single decision layer, (ii) a context-aware semantic cache, and (iii) a design that constrains agent behaviour through controlled tool calls. We measure classification accuracy, retrieval and answer quality, and the effect of caching on computational load. The results show that moving routing onto a local model and constraining agents through tools reduces unnecessary LLM calls while preserving answer quality.
