AB  - Video-based instruction has emerged as a predominant course delivery mode in technology-enabled learning systems; still, the issue of long, unstructured lecture recordings remains a concern for students. While recent methods incorporate keyword matching and manual forwarding, they fall short in terms of precision. In this paper, a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) assistant is designed for use in multi-video lecture settings. Whisper is used to convert lecture videos into audio files and time-stamped transcripts. These transcripts are broken into focused segments and indexed through a compact local model served via Ollama. A grounding-aware LLM is used to provide timely and reliable explanations, tying in exactly to the relevant time frames. Questions are posted in a shared embedding space, where the best matches are identified using cosine similarity. A local language model then generates an answer while filtering out unsupported information. The model is evaluated using twenty lecture questions; the assistant retrieved the correct top answer about 90% of the time, and every correct answer appeared within its top three or top five suggestions. The system uses three strategies for extracting information: (i) if the topic is located in one place, it retrieves that segment from the corresponding video; (ii) if the topic appears in more than one lecture, it retrieves segments from multiple videos; and (iii) if the topic is not present in the material, the model reports “Not covered,” which prevents misleading learning. This study presents a RAG-based teaching assistant that is both efficient and reliable, and that serves as a practical navigation tool for educational video repositories. The proposed RAG-based Teaching Assistant is practical and reproducible, and it significantly improves student productivity while supporting more precise revision.
