AB  - One of the challenges of automated system’s understanding of skin diseases is lack of medical knowledge from visual perception. There are two types of image based models, which detect the lesions, and knowledge based models, which store verified information, but lack the visual analysis. To solve this, we present a retrieval-augmented multimodal framework that fuses dermoscopic image analysis and medical knowledge retrieval based on documents. Architecture includes an efficientNetB0 vision classifier trained with HAM10000, a vector database using FAISS for semantic retrieval, and a LLM (Mistral-7B) with a retrieval-augmented chain-of-thought for generation of explanations. This classifier is used for 7 dermatological disease classification using softmax confidence scores. The balanced accuracy is 77.14% and the macro-averaged F1-score is 0.77, with good results for the classes that are quite different in the visual domain and moderate results for the classes that are quite similar in the structural domain. Semantic search uses a predicted labels/queries by the user and extracts the contextual evidence, the LLM delivers the structured explanations in an overview of the disease, symptoms, precautions and disease management. The system has been adapted as a web-based Chatbot system for image-based diagnosis and medical question answering. To measure the Quantitative RAG, the 20 question/answer pairs are evaluated using Answer Relevance, Faithfulness and Context Relevance, which are grounded and reliable with RAG scores of 0.72, 0.63 and 0.65, respectively.
